<section>
  <h2>What Remains Unproven</h2>

  <p>The staged tier rests, on Safari specifically, on three assumptions stacked on one
  another. None has been verified, and — notably — none appears to have been verified publicly
  by anyone.</p>

  {{ c.warn('Nobody has published this',
     'An extensive search found no blog post, bug report, benchmark or library issue in which
      anyone stages a multi-gigabyte file in private origin storage on real Safari and reports
      what the export step costs. The one discussion thread that proposes exactly this
      technique has its author stating plainly that he never tested Safari. The absence of
      evidence is itself the finding.') }}

  <table class="num">
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Assumption</th><th>If it is wrong</th></tr></thead>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Safari's file handle returns a lazy, disk-backed object rather than copying into
          memory.</td>
      <td>The entire Safari case collapses; Safari falls back to the handoff tier.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>Exporting a gigabyte-scale staged file actually completes on macOS and iPhone.</td>
      <td>Same, plus a failure that may be silent rather than reported.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td>The roughly 100 MB page-memory ceiling on iPhone does not apply to this storage
          traffic.</td>
      <td>The staged tier is unusable on iPhone at any meaningful size.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Also open</h3>

  <p>Whether Chrome for Android's four launch defects have been fixed. Whether persistent
  storage reliably exempts a paused job from Safari's seven-day expiry. Memory ceilings for
  Android browsers, for which no current figures appear to exist publicly. And whether other
  Chromium-derived Android browsers inherited the folder-access change.</p>

  <h3>How these get answered</h3>

  <p>Not by more reading. Assumptions one to three need real Apple hardware, because the
  automated tooling used for this investigation cannot drive the real browser. The Android
  defects need a real Android device, and are the easiest to settle: a probe page exists that
  reports the automated portion and prompts for the two observations only a person can make —
  whether the folder picker responds promptly on a large photo folder, and whether saving
  offers a filename or only an overwrite.</p>

  <p>Two device checks and one product decision would close this investigation. Further
  literature search has reached diminishing returns; the same walls were hit repeatedly, and
  the gaps that remain are gaps in what has been published, not in what has been read.</p>
</section>
